.\"	segalloc.2: the 286 port's own
.\"
.TH SEGALLOC 2 "November 20, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
segalloc, segsize, segfree \- more data segments for a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <segment.h>
.PP
.ft B
void __far *segalloc(size)
long size;
.PP
.ft B
int segsize(seg, size)
void __far *seg;
long size;
.PP
.ft B
int segfree(seg)
void __far *seg;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A process's memory is a list of segments, each of 64K bytes at most.
A program comes with its code and its data, and in the small and
medium memory models its data, stack and heap are one segment
together, which
.IR sbrk (2)
makes larger up to that size and no further.
These calls give a process segments besides.
.PP
.I Segalloc
makes a segment of
.I size
bytes, all zero, and returns the far address of its first byte:
its selector, with an offset of 0.
A program of any memory model reaches it through a far pointer,
declared with
.B __far
before the
.BR * ,
which is four bytes where another pointer is two.
A reference past the end of a segment is a memory violation,
as one past the break is.
.PP
If
.I size
is more than 65536 the piece is as many segments as it takes,
each of them full but the last.
Their selectors follow one another, each the one before it and 8,
so byte
.I n
of the piece is at offset
.IR n " % 65536"
of the selector that is
.RI "8 * (" n " / 65536)"
more than the first;
the macro
.B SEGADDR(seg, n)
is that address.
An offset does not carry into the selector:
adding to a far pointer changes its offset alone.
.PP
.I Segsize
makes the piece that starts at
.I seg
.I size
bytes long.
What it gains is zero, and what it keeps is as it was.
A piece that is to pass a multiple of 65536 bytes
needs the selectors after its own to be free.
.PP
.I Segfree
gives the piece that starts at
.I seg
back to the system.
.PP
.I Seg
is the address that
.I segalloc
returned.
What these calls make is the process's own, as its data is:
a child made by
.IR fork (2)
has a copy of each, and they are gone when the process executes
another program or exits.
A process has room for 31 segments in all,
its program's among them,
and its segments together may be no more than the system
lets a process have (2 megabytes as distributed).
.SH "RETURN VALUE
.I Segalloc
returns the address of the new piece, or a null pointer,
with the reason in
.IR errno .
.I Segsize
and
.I segfree
return 0, or \-1 with the reason in
.IR errno .
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
.I Size
is not more than 0;
or
.I seg
is not the start of something
.I segalloc
made.
.TP 15
[ENOMEM]
The process has no selectors free for the piece,
or not that many in a row,
or those after it are in use;
or it would have more memory than a process may.
.TP 15
[EBUSY]
The process's stack is in what
.I segfree
was to free.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
brk(2), execve(2), fork(2), malloc(3)
.SH BUGS
A segment is where it is: the system cannot move a piece to
selectors that have room for it to grow.
